BT /F2 12 Tf 58.00 765.00 Td (How Search works in a Skip List) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 744.40 Td (The search begins at the top layer and moves horizontally along its linked list. If the target element is found) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 731.00 Td (there, the search stops immediately and returns, this is exactly why the sparse top layer is useful: finding the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 717.60 Td (element early, at a sparser level, means fewer nodes had to be checked, keeping the time complexity low.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 697.70 Td (Searching in a skip list happens like this:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 677.80 Td (When searching for k:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 657.90 Td (If k = key, done!) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 638.00 Td (If k < next key, go down a level.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 618.10 Td (If k >= next key, go right.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 598.20 Td (This is what gives skip lists their efficiency: Skip lists offer O\(log n\) average complexity for both search and) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 584.80 Td (insertion/deletion.) Tj ET
